==English==
{{wikipedia}}
[[Image:Citation2.jpg|thumb|250px|A '''quotation''' of French text.]]

===Etymology===
The obsolete sense of “quota”, from {{bor|en|ML.|quotatio}}, from {{der|en|la|quotāre}}, is attested from the 15th century. The sense “fragment of verbal expression”, attested from the 17th century, may come from this source, or else from the verb {{affix|en|quote|-ation}}.<ref>{{R:Etymonline|quotation}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|en|/kwoʊˈteɪʃən/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-quotation.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪʃən|s=3}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# A fragment of a human expression that is repeated by somebody else, for example from [[literature]] or a famous [[speech]].
#: {{syn|en|quote|cite|citation}}
#: {{ux|en|"Where they burn books, they will also burn people" is a famous '''quotation''' from {{w|Heinrich Heine}}.}}
# A price that has been quoted for buying or selling.
#: {{ux|en|Let's get a '''quotation''' for repairing the roof before we decide whether it's worth doing.}}
# The act of setting a [[price]].
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[quota]], a [[share]].<ref>{{R:Webster 1913|Quotation}}</ref>

